10. Completion criteria
- [ ] All in-scope systems and software have a private inventory entry.
- [ ] Intended and prohibited network paths have been verified.
- [ ] Exposure and privileged-access reviews have been completed.
- [ ] Monitoring and notifications have been exercised safely.
- [ ] Repository and dependency reviews have been performed.
- [ ] A representative backup restore has passed.
- [ ] Incident-response contacts, evidence handling, and recovery steps are documented privately.
- [ ] Findings have owners, remediation dates, and retest plans.
Important: Checking these boxes means the audit activities were performed; it is not a guarantee that the environment is free of vulnerabilities.
11. Public publishing checklist
Before every commit or release:
- [ ] Search text and filenames for real IPs, domains, hostnames, usernames, emails, and file paths.
- [ ] Confirm no tokens, passwords, private keys, .env files, or full configuration exports are included.
- [ ] Strip screenshots and logs of private details, including metadata where applicable.
- [ ] Confirm no specific firewall policies, private VLAN IDs, remote-access routes, or sensitive findings are disclosed.
- [ ] Review staged changes and commit history for accidental exposure.
- [ ] Keep real audit results in private documentation, not public issues or pull requests.
